-- Unbounded recursion must produce a catchable `stack overflow` diagnostic,
-- not a native stack overflow. Before the depth guard in
-- `saule_interpreter::eval::DepthGuard` this aborted the process with
-- SIGABRT — no span, no message, and no chance for `try`/`catch` to run.
--
-- The recursive call is deliberately **not** in tail position. It used to be
-- (`return forever(n + 1)`), and tail calls turned this fixture from a test
-- into a hang: a tail-recursive loop is bounded by nothing, so both engines
-- span forever and `run_tests.sh`, which has no per-fixture timeout, never
-- finished. Binding the result first is what keeps a frame alive per level,
-- which is the thing being tested. Do not "simplify" it back.
local fn forever(n: integer) -> integer
	local deeper: integer = forever(n + 1)
	return deeper
end

class Main
	static fn main()
		println(forever(0))
	end
end
